levi 2.0.0
==========

First Bioconductor release of the rewritten package. The changes below were
developed as internal versions 3.10.0 and 3.11.x and are released together as
levi 2.0.0; internal version numbers quoted in older notes refer to this release.

Landscape core (degree weighting, 2026-09-16)
----------------------------------------------
    o New argument edge_weighting in levi(), passed through by the
      replicate and single-cell landscape tests. "midpoint" (default)
      keeps the historical deposit, one support point of weight one per
      edge midpoint, which weights the landscape implicitly by degree.
      "degree" weights the midpoint of edge (i, j) by (1/d_i + 1/d_j)/2,
      so the midpoints around any node add up to one; "none" smooths the
      node values alone. landscape_gauss() gains a per-point weights
      vector. The choice is recorded in metadata$edge_weighting and the
      weights in metadata$support_weights, so the sample-label null is
      built with the same weighting as the observed landscape.
      inst/scripts/12-degree-weighting.R compares calibration, power and
      layout sensitivity of the three options.
    o The grid always leaves room for the smoothed silhouette. The margin
      around the network used to be the zoom alone (10% of the network
      extent by default), while the Gaussian kernel at the default
      smoothing carries occupancy about 20% beyond the outermost node, so
      the landscape was clipped on every side where a node sat near the
      border and regions touching the frame lost part of their mass. The
      silhouette is now measured once on a generous grid and the frame is
      set to fit it; zoomValueInput adds up to 20% on top (0 = widest,
      100 = frame that just fits). Observed and null landscapes share the
      grid, so p-values are unchanged where nothing was clipped.
    o The landscapes drawn by leviReplicateInference() and
      leviSingleCellRegionalInference() are titled "logFC" instead of
      ".logFC", the name of an internal column.
    o Vignettes: levi.Rmd recommends leviReplicateInference() and
      leviGraphTFCEInference() next to the node-label test, with a
      worked example; the reporting checklist in levi_inference.Rmd gains
      the same item.
    o Shiny interface: the 2D landscape and the 3D surface are shown in
      two tabs, so either figure takes the full width; ticking "3D
      surface" brings the 3D tab to the front. The format selector,
      "Download Plot" and the expression area belong to the 2D tab, the
      HTML download to the 3D tab. The gene, node score, peak and region
      tables are tabs of their own below the figure; "Genes" lists what
      was brushed on the map and is hidden while the 3D tab is in front.
    o The GUI section of vignette("levi") is rewritten around two new
      screenshots (2D map with a brushed selection; Settings tab, 3D
      surface and regions table) and documents every control of the
      side panel and every tab of the results. The GUI summary in
      vignette("levi_guide") is updated to match.
    o Integration tests (tests/testthat/test_integration_real_data.R)
      replay the real-data analyses of the supplement, airway, GSE10072
      and Kang 2018 on STRING and KEGG networks, and check that their
      conclusions hold. They are skipped unless LEVI_INTEGRATION is set;
      see inst/scripts/README.md.

Inference layer (technical review, 2026-09-06)
----------------------------------------------
    o Single-cell pseudobulk tests drop genes with fewer than 10 counts in
      two pseudobulks before TMM normalisation and the limma-trend fit;
      network genes are always kept. Fitting the tens of thousands of
      all-zero droplet rows left more than half of the residual variances
      at exactly zero, which made the eBayes prior and variance trend
      unreliable for the genes under test and raised a warning on every
      permutation (Kang 2018 PBMC data, 35,635 genes).
    o Regional figures label only the regions that pass sig_level, in
      script mode, in the replicate and single-cell tests and in the Shiny
      interface. Every region used to carry a "p = 1.000" box. Without a
      permutation test the descriptive regions keep their name labels.
    o Sample-label permutations count the possible arrangements with
      choose() before deciding between the exact and Monte Carlo schemes.
      Enumerating them first aborted leviReplicateInference() and the graph
      tests on unblocked designs with more than about 30 samples
      ("vector size cannot be NA"), e.g. 58 tumours vs 49 normals.
    o New leviSave3D() writes the 3D surface to html, png, jpeg, webp, svg,
      pdf or tiff from a chosen camera position, so the same viewpoint can
      be reproduced across runs. Static formats go through plotly and the
      kaleido Python package; tiff is converted with magick. The 3D surface
      in the Shiny interface gains a "Download 3D (HTML)" button that keeps
      the view the user rotated to, and the toolbar PNG export was raised to
      1600 x 1200 at scale 2.
    o Shiny interface: the highlight box accepts several genes, draws one
      circle per gene at its own grid cell (it used to paint the whole region
      nearest to the gene) and offers a colour selector; Multicolor / Two
      colors became a radio button since they are mutually exclusive.
    o The Shiny interface runs the regional test by default (a "Test unit"
      selector keeps the legacy cell-wise contours), outlines and labels the
      significant regions like script mode and adds a downloadable regions
      table. A parity test checks GUI and script agree on regional p-values.
    o New vignette "levi: Calibration and a real dataset". Type I error,
      power and layout sensitivity were simulated offline
      (inst/scripts/10-calibration.R) and ship as small CSV summaries; the
      airway dexamethasone experiment was preprocessed offline
      (inst/scripts/11-airway-preprocess.R) into a few text files, so the
      vignette builds in well under a minute.
    o leviBulkGraphInference() keeps the observed size factors and
      dispersions fixed across permutations by default (refit_dispersions =
      FALSE), refitting only the GLM; refit_dispersions = TRUE restores the
      full re-estimation. The documentation points to
      leviGraphClusterInference() on voom/logCPM data as the fast route.
    o Moran, Getis-Ord, weighted topology and spectrum statistics use sparse
      adjacency matrices (Matrix moved to Imports). leviGraphSpectrum()
      keeps the dense eigendecomposition (partial solvers were measured
      slower for a quarter of the spectrum) and now documents its O(n^3)
      cost and pathway-scale scope.
    o Document the implicit degree weighting of the landscape (edge midpoints
      as support points) in ?levi and in the main vignette; note that the
      Getis-Ord Gi* of a node adjacent to every other node is NA.
    o Examples use designs that can reach alpha = 0.05; the tests assert the
      insufficient-permutations warning on their deliberately small designs.
    o inference_unit = "region" is now the default of levi(); the per-cell
      test remains available as inference_unit = "cell" and is what the
      Shiny interface uses for its contours.
    o New vignette "levi: Which null answers which question" contrasting the
      node-label, sample-label and rewiring nulls, the p-value floor of small
      designs and the BPPARAM interface.
    o Title, Description and biocViews describe the inference layer;
      Normalization and PeakDetection were dropped, RNASeq, GraphAndNetwork
      and StatisticalMethod added.
    o Version set to 1.99.0 on the Bioconductor devel branch so that the
      Bioconductor core team assigns 2.0.0 at release.
    o Document every argument of the inference functions and replace the
      formals() placeholder examples by runnable ones on the hub dataset.
    o Factor the permutation tests around one engine (R/leviPermutationEngine.R)
      with a BPPARAM argument; the default SerialParam runs as before, and
      MulticoreParam()/SnowParam() parallelise the null distribution with
      identical results because all randomness stays in the calling process.
    o Warn when the design cannot reach the significance level: with fewer
      than 1/alpha label arrangements (or Monte Carlo draws) no result can be
      significant, e.g. 3 vs 3 unblocked gives a floor of 0.05 and 4 paired
      donors a floor of 0.0625.
    o Blocks that hold a single condition no longer error; they contribute one
      fixed arrangement to the permutation space.
    o leviGraphGetisOrd() reports PTwoSided (twice the smaller one-sided
      p-value) and a BH-adjusted PAdjusted across nodes; Significant now uses
      the adjusted value. Previously it flagged min(POver, PUnder) <= 0.05,
      a two-sided test at the 10% level without multiplicity control.
      leviGraphMoran() adds PAdjusted to the local table.
    o leviGraphSpectrum(): fix the low-frequency energy fraction, which took
      the first eigenvectors of the Laplacian (the highest frequencies, since
      eigen() sorts decreasingly) instead of the last. LowFrequency and
      LowFrequencyP change. The eigendecomposition is now computed once.
    o leviGraphMoran() derives global and local nulls from the same
      permutations instead of two independent sets.
    o Pseudobulk single-cell tests use TMM-normalised log2 CPM (edgeR) and the
      limma-trend moderated t; normalize = "none" restores plain CPM.
    o leviBulkGraphInference() uses one DESeq2 dispersion estimator for the
      observed statistic and every permutation; if the trend fit fails in any
      draw, everything is recomputed with gene-wise dispersions and the
      choice is returned as dispersion_estimator. A message states the cost
      of refitting DESeq2 per permutation.
    o leviSingleCellInteractionTFCE() locates the interaction coefficient by
      its exact design column name and errors if it is absent, instead of a
      pattern match that failed for cell types with spaces in their names.
    o Add leviReplicateInference() for regional maximum-mass inference by
      permuting biological sample labels, optionally within blocks.
    o Add leviAdjustPathways() to correct regional results across a family of
      pathway networks and leviRegionGenes() to rank gene contributions.
    o Add optional perm_strata so node signals can be shuffled within bins of
      expression, detection rate or network degree.
    o Add inference_unit = "region": maximum excess-mass permutation test
      over eight-connected areas and both directions; retain null maxima,
      report spatial p-values per area and draw regional 2D boundaries.
      Inference is conditional on network/layout, not biological replication.
      Cell inference remains available as the legacy default.
    o Correct medusa validation to linear-scale ratio and a matched smoking
      category; save a region-focused figure and reproducible results.
    o Add explicit eight-connected landscape regions with direction, area,
      mass, peak, centroid and member cells. Without permutations they remain
      descriptive regions.
    o Use the same transformed signal for surfaces, scores, peaks and tests.
      Ratio scores now preserve the absolute Test/(Test+Control) scale.
    o Share the full numerical pipeline between Shiny and scripts and use
      expressionLog only to back-transform ratio inputs.
    o Permute measured node pairs and recalculate edge signals; adjust both
      directional cell families jointly with BY by default. Raw p-values
      remain available separately. Inferential calibration is still pending.
    o Record result provenance and reject incompatible leviDiff comparisons.
    o Fix edgeR test-object conversion and KEGG background identifier mapping.
    o Correct signal contracts and adapter examples in documentation.

Landscape rebuilt on normalised convolution
-------------------------------------------
* The landscape is now computed as a Gaussian-kernel weighted average
  (Nadaraya-Watson normalised convolution) instead of an unweighted mean over
  the k nearest points:

      S(x) = sum_i w_i(x) s_i / sum_i w_i(x),  w_i(x) = exp(-||x-p_i||^2/2s^2)

  Dividing by the sum of the weights makes every landscape value a convex
  combination of the input signals, so the result always lies within
  [min s_i, max s_i]. Data with no variation now yields the neutral value 0.5
  for any smoothing level and any network density.

  Previously, matrix_entrada injected a synthetic point with signal 0 at every
  background grid cell, and those points entered the k-NN average. Because
  background cells vastly outnumber network points, the landscape amplitude was
  divided by k: a node whose true signal was 1.0 scored exactly 1/k. With the
  default smoothing (k = 9) the whole landscape was compressed into the bottom
  11% of the colour scale, result$scores was off the documented [0,1] scale, and
  the peak threshold in .detectPeaks2D was unreachable, so result$peaks could
  report valleys but never a peak on sparse networks.

  Verified against the Expected results sections of the bundled toy datasets:
  10 of 10 assertions now pass; 3 of 10 passed before.

* The network silhouette is now carried by the denominator sum_i w_i(x), which
  measures network occupancy, rather than by zero-signal filler points. Cells
  outside the network return NA and are drawn with a background colour. "No
  network here" and "network present, minimal expression" are no longer the
  same value.

* Much faster, because the Gaussian kernel is separable: the 2-D convolution is
  computed as two 1-D passes, O(res^2 * r) instead of O(res^2 * n), and the
  point count only enters an O(n) deposit step. medusa.dat at resolution 135
  went from 28.5 s to 11.5 ms. The cost no longer grows with network size: a
  50000-point network at resolution 240 takes 40 ms.

* Permutation test: only the signals are shuffled, never the coordinates, so the
  silhouette and the convolution denominator are computed once instead of once
  per iteration. p-values now use (k+1)/(n+1) rather than k/n, which avoids
  reporting a p-value of exactly 0.

* SigCoordPiso(), matrix_entrada() and matrix_saida() were removed and replaced
  by landscape_gauss(). nearest_node_grid() was added to label each grid cell
  with its closest node, which is what the GUI region selection needs.

* GUI (LEVIui): the Shiny app no longer compiles its own copies of the C++ core
  with cppFunction() at startup. It calls the same compiled routines as script
  mode, so no compiler toolchain is needed to launch the interface.

  Sharing the compiled core was not enough to keep the two paths in step: the R
  pipeline around it was still written twice, and it had already drifted. The
  "Pink & Green" palette was #f757ca/#04ff00 in the interface against
  #e854d9/#90db56 in script mode, so the same option produced different
  figures depending on how levi was called, and the scale arrows sat at
  different heights on each side. The interface now also shares
  .parseNetwork(), .permutationPvalues(), .colorSet() and
  .buildLandscapeChart(), which removes its private copies of network parsing,
  the permutation test, the palettes and the figure itself -- about 170 lines,
  taking app.R from 1109 to 967.

  The interface also gained the 3D surface, which only script mode had: a
  "3D surface" box under the plot options renders it through the same
  .buildSurface3D(), significance boundary included. LEVIui's help had been
  claiming the interface exposed everything script mode offers. plotly stays a
  suggested dependency, so both the control and the output are left out when it
  is not installed rather than stopping the app from starting. tests/testthat/test_gui_parity.R runs both paths on the same
  input and requires the same scores, the same surface and, under a fixed seed,
  the same p-values, so a future divergence fails there instead of going
  unnoticed.

GUI fixes
---------
* The interface could not compute anything under igraph >= 2.0. It called
  graph.edgelist(), deprecated there, and wrapped the whole computation in a
  tryCatch() that also caught warnings. tryCatch unwinds the stack, so the
  deprecation notice aborted the run, and the user was told "Incorrect file
  format" -- a message about the one thing that was not wrong. igraph 2.0 was
  released in early 2024 and the app had no tests, so nothing reported it.
  It now calls graph_from_edgelist(), the name script mode already used.
* Warnings no longer abort anything. They are raised through
  withCallingHandlers, shown as a notification, and whatever was running
  continues. Every warning used to be fatal, including the ones added in this
  release for negative values in ratio mode. Two other places had the same
  pattern: reading the expression file, where "incomplete final line found" --
  a file whose last line has no newline, which read.table parses correctly --
  was reported as "Incorrect file format" and the data thrown away, and the
  brush selection on the map.
* tests/testthat/test_gui_parity.R drives the server through shiny::testServer:
  files in, scores out, agreement with script mode, and a run that survives a
  warning. The interface had no end-to-end coverage at all.

Guards on the input values
--------------------------
* signal_mode = "ratio" now warns when the expression data contain negative
  values. Test/(Test + Control) is only meaningful on a non-negative linear
  scale: with signed input it maps a strongly down-regulated gene onto the same
  score as a strongly up-regulated one. Following the documented workflow for a
  differential expression table but forgetting signal_mode = "logfc" used to
  return an inverted landscape (Spearman -0.52 against the correct result),
  with genes at logFC -5.4 scoring 0.99, and said nothing.
* Test + Control == 0 no longer escapes as +/-Inf. Those values are undefined,
  so they are reported and set to the neutral score 0.5.
* The [0, 1] range of LandscapeScore is now enforced for every signal mode in
  the one place they all pass through, and fixed by a test. It used to hold
  only by construction, which stopped being true once a value could be
  negative.
* Duplicated identifiers in the expression data are still averaged, but the
  number collapsed is now reported. Two contradictory measurements average to
  the neutral point, which is indistinguishable from a gene that did not
  change.

New in this release
-------------------
* leviFromEdges(): builds a levi-ready network from a plain list of
  interactions. levi draws over the plane the network occupies, so it needs a
  coordinate per node, and until now the only way to obtain one without
  supplying it by hand was leviFromSTRING(), which needs the STRING database
  and network access. Anyone arriving with an edge list -- which is what most
  interaction databases and most collaborators hand over -- had to go through
  another tool first. The layout is computed with igraph (fr, kk, lgl, dh or
  circle) and scaled to the range levi expects. It accepts a data.frame, a
  matrix or an igraph graph, and reports interactions dropped for a missing
  endpoint. leviFromSTRING() now shares the same layout routine.
* The 3D surface now carries the significance boundary. When n_perm > 0 the 2D
  figure has always been overlaid with the p = sig_level contours, while the
  plotly view showed the bare surface: the one piece of information saying
  which part of the landscape holds up against the null was dropped, with
  nothing on the figure admitting the omission. The boundary is now traced onto
  the surface itself, solid for over-expression and dotted for under-expression,
  matching the 2D line styles.
* levi() now returns an object of class "levi_result" instead of a bare list,
  and a print() method summarises it: the comparison, how many nodes were
  scored, the score range, the strongest and weakest genes, the peaks and
  valleys found, and whether a permutation test ran. Inspecting a result no
  longer requires knowing its six fields by heart. The fields themselves are
  unchanged, so result$scores and the rest keep working.

Parameter changes
-----------------
* levi(): the argument geneSymbolnput was renamed to geneSymbolInput, fixing a
  typo that had been part of the public API. This is a breaking change and no
  alias is kept: calls using the old spelling now fail with "unused argument".
  The new name occupies the same position in the signature, so positional calls
  are unaffected. Note that the old spelling never had a rescue through R's
  partial matching either, because geneSymbolInput is not a prefix of
  geneSymbolnput -- anyone typing the natural spelling already got an error.
* contrastValueInput and smoothValueInput keep their 0-100 public range but are
  remapped internally: contrast now sets the occupancy threshold for the
  silhouette, and smoothing now sets sigma, the Gaussian width in grid cells.
  Sigma is scaled relative to resolutionValueInput, so changing the resolution
  no longer changes the apparent smoothing.
* smoothValueInput no longer affects the value scale, only the level of detail.
* The max() normalisation applied to matrixOutExp and matrixOutCtrl was dropped.
  It existed to undo the dilution described above and would now destroy the
  absolute meaning of the scale; it had never been applied to the combined
  matrix in any released version.

Documentation
-------------
* inst/scripts/: a series of eight self-contained example scripts, from
  loading the input files through interpreting the result, plus a README
  indexing them. They use only the bundled datasets, so they run with no
  download and no setup. Reach them with
  system.file("scripts", package = "levi").

  01 first steps and the anatomy of the returned object
  02 reading the landscape: scale, neutral point, silhouette, peaks
  03 how network topology shapes the result, across five topologies
  04 signal_mode, logfc_k, expressionLog and single-column input
  05 batch mode, leviGrid() and leviDiff()
  06 permutation test, p-value matrices and contours
  07 SummarizedExperiment, ExpressionSet, DE tables and STRING
  08 resolution, smoothing, contrast, zoom, palettes and export

Bug fixes
---------
* Contour layers no longer warn about the NA background. Both the
  contourLevi overlay and the permutation contours now drop out-of-silhouette
  cells before reaching stat_contour(). When every p-value falls on the same
  side of sig_level there is no curve to draw, and levi now says so instead of
  emitting ggplot's "Zero contours were generated".

Note
----
* Every landscape changes. Figures in the vignettes and in Readme.md need to be
  regenerated.

Adapters, analysis helpers and GUI (formerly internal 3.10.0)
-------------------------------------------------------------
* n_perm / sig_level / perm_side parameters: bilateral permutation test.
  perm_side = "both" (default): dashed contour for over-expression AND dotted
  contour for under-expression. perm_side = "over" or "under" for one-sided.
  result$pvalues is a list with $over and $under matrices.
  GUI (LEVIui): Settings tab exposes n_perm, perm_side, sig_level controls.
* Peak/valley table exposed in GUI (LEVIui) below the score table after Run,
  with "Download CSV" button. Score table also has a "Download CSV" button.
* leviFromSTRING(): build a levi-compatible network directly from the STRING
  database (requires the STRINGdb Bioconductor package). Supply a character
  vector of gene symbols; the function maps them to STRING IDs, downloads
  interactions above score_threshold, computes an igraph layout (fr/kk/lgl/
  dh/circle), and returns $nodes and $edges data.frames ready to pass to
  levi() with fileTypeInput = "stg". No manual file download required.
  stg branch in levi_function now accepts data.frames directly (like
  expressionInput already did), enabling fully in-memory workflows.
* leviFromSE(): convert SummarizedExperiment objects directly to levi input.
  Supports condition labels via colData or explicit sample name/index selection.
* leviGrid(): arrange multiple levi() results side by side for comparison.
  Uses patchwork, cowplot, or gridExtra (whichever is installed).
* leviDiff(): subtract two landscapes cell-by-cell; generates a diverging
  blue-to-red map of regional expression change between two conditions.
  Requires $landscape field (available from levi >= 2.0.0).
* leviEnrich(): GO (BP/MF/CC) and KEGG enrichment on peak genes (top-N) and
  valley genes (bottom-N) from result$scores. Requires clusterProfiler.
* GUI (LEVIui): gene search box — type a gene name to highlight its position
  on the landscape with a yellow circle.
* GUI (LEVIui): "Label peaks on map" checkbox overlays gene names at detected
  peak/valley positions (uses ggrepel if installed).
* GUI (LEVIui): permutation test shows a progress bar with per-iteration
  feedback ("Permutation k / n").
* leviDiff() and leviGrid() use the null-coalescing operator %||% internally.
* signal_mode parameter: choose how raw expression values are mapped to the
  landscape score in [0,1]:
    "ratio"  (default) Test/(Test+Control) + min-max. Best for counts, TPM,
             FPKM, linear proteomics LFQ.
    "logfc"  Sigmoid 1/(1+exp(-k*logFC)). Best for RMA microarray, VST/rlog,
             log2-proteomics, single-cell avg_log2FC (single-column mode).
             logfc_k parameter controls steepness (default 1; use 0.5 for
             large-FC datasets such as scRNA-seq, 2 for tight-FC microarray).
    "zscore" Z-scores the logFC distribution then applies pnorm(). Best for
             multi-condition comparisons or heterogeneous-scale datasets.
  For single-column logFC input (DESeq2 LFC only, edgeR logFC only, Seurat
  avg_log2FC): use readExpColumn("logFC-logFC") + signal_mode="logfc".
  NA values are replaced with 0.5 (ratio) or 0.0 (logfc/zscore) to preserve
  the "no change = neutral" semantics. Non-ratio modes use range-based
  rescaling for C++ compatibility.
  GUI (LEVIui): Settings tab exposes signal_mode dropdown and logfc_k input.
* GUI (LEVIui): Shiny app architecture refactored — three separate
  observeEvent(input$action) blocks consolidated into one; output$graph and
  all DT tables declared at server level (reactive, not re-assigned on each
  Run click); reactiveValues fully initialised at startup; v$currentPlot
  stores the complete plot (with contours) for download.
* Download plot now includes permutation contours when present.
* Fixed deprecated size= aesthetic in ggplot2 annotate() segments
  (replaced with linewidth=).
* Node landscape scores: levi() now returns result$scores, a ranked data.frame
  with LandscapeScore (0-1) per gene/node. Scores close to 1 indicate
  strong over-expression; scores close to 0 indicate under-expression.
* Automatic peak/valley detection: result$peaks lists local maxima (over-
  expressed regions) and minima (under-expressed regions) with the nearest
  network gene, matrix position, and score.
* plot3d parameter: when plotly is installed, levi() produces an interactive
  3D surface alongside the standard 2D landscape.
* Direct data.frame/matrix input: expressionInput now accepts data.frame or
  matrix objects directly, in addition to file paths.
* DE tool integration: leviFromDESeq2(), leviFromEdgeR(), leviFromLimma(),
  leviFromSeurat() convert differential expression results directly into
  levi-compatible data.frames.
* Two toy datasets added to inst/extdata (hub_network/hub_expression for star
  topology; gradient_network/gradient_expression for linear chain) with known
  expected results and corresponding testthat tests.
* Binder configuration (runtime.txt, install.R) for web-based interactive demo.

Bug fixes
---------
* Fixed operator precedence: 3:delimiter-1 corrected to 3:(delimiter-1).
* Fixed C++ buffer overflow in matrix_saida and matrixOutFun when
  smoothValue >= h.
* Fixed colorSet() returning inside else block (implicit return).
* Removed duplicate coord_fixed() call in ggplot section.
* Moved cppFunction() calls in Shiny app to module level (compiled once).
* Fixed expressionLog: 2^ back-transform now only applied for signal_mode =
  "ratio"; when signal_mode = "logfc" or "zscore", expressionLog = TRUE is
  silently ignored (a message is emitted) because log-scale data must remain
  in log2 for logFC computation. Previously, back-transforming before logFC
  would yield linear differences (e.g., 2^8 − 2^5 = 224) instead of the
  correct log-difference (8 − 5 = 3).
* Fixed internal parameter name inconsistency: networkNodesInput renamed to
  networkCoordinatesInput in levi_function.R to match the public API.
* NAMESPACE: all broad import(package) directives replaced with specific
  importFrom(package, function) declarations, eliminating BiocCheck ERROR.
  Packages only used in inst/shiny/app.R (shinydashboard, shinyjs, DT) and
  packages not used in R/ code (RColorBrewer, httr, colorspace) removed from
  NAMESPACE. knitr and rmarkdown moved from Imports to Suggests (VignetteBuilder
  only). BiocStyle moved to Suggests.
* .gitignore added: src/*.so, src/*.o, vignettes/*.html, vignettes/*.R,
  inst/doc/ excluded from version control as required by Bioconductor.
* DAT parser: added informative message when fewer than 1 edge is found,
  guiding users to duplicate the first edge on rows 2 and 3 (known parser
  behaviour where the row-2 entry is skipped).
* Added integration tests: leviGrid, leviDiff, leviFromSE, leviFromDESeq2,
  leviFromLimma, and all three signal_mode values now covered by testthat.

levi 1.0.0
==========
* first version of levi
